<header><p class="enc-kicker">Work / monument · infrastructure</p><h1 class="enc-title">Guayaquil and Quito Railway</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1908">1908</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/20th-century">20th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/guayaquil">Guayaquil</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/quito">Quito</a> Railway links the port of Guayaquil with Ecuador's capital, Quito, across the Andes. Promoted by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/eloy-alfaro">Eloy Alfaro</a> and built largely by American contractors with many Jamaican workers, it was completed in 1908, including the steep switchbacks of the Devil's Nose. It joined the coast and highlands economically, and in later decades it served mainly as a tourist line.</p></div>
